(project pricklypear-local-first
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: local-first — a local lens is a branch of the one tree whose root lives on the client. The pad detaches, roots independently, and grafts back. This revisits remote-eval's 'no second store on the client' hard-out: the hard-out was written against an uncoordinated client copy; a lens is a governed copy whose merge semantics already exist (three-way tree-diff, branching.borg / one-tree.branching-interaction). Same Prim_call seam, same tree, different lens policy.")
 (status planned)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-14 chapter created from the local-first design discussion. Thesis: one-tree + user-owned data + three-way tree-diff merge are, almost without addition, the local-first primitives. The 2026-08-02 /data revision (data.u.<user>.*) was made for multi-user isolation and turns out to also fix the replication unit — the isolation boundary and the replication boundary are the same shape. remote-eval is the online-remote mode (local compute, remote data plane); local-first is the offline-capable mode (local lens, sync on reconnect). One seam, two policies. The cactus metaphor (pads = lenses, grafting = merge, glochids = sync conflicts) is evocative and correct but is NOT load-bearing in this spec text — it lives in prose, not the source of truth.

 2026-12 campaign 8.0 plan pass: lens-model + replication-units pinned for the 8.1 make (details CONTRACT blocks below). A lens = a CHILD BRANCH of the checked-out branch via (fork) / Store.fork_branch (P5 empty-lens fork); the server branch stays the parent; single-writer-per-lens pins to the branch model; code sync = branch-pointer move (promote/merge), data sync = in-place reconcile. Replication unit = data.u.user, data.shared, b.lens plus branch ancestry; path-prefix RLS confinement is the replication boundary. Sync-negotiation handle = the branch,generation coordinate — the same migration-017 branch_generations / gen-now clock remote-eval.caching already keys on (one counter, three consumers). Status stays planned; the 8.1 make flips.|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section local-first
  (doc "A client process that holds a durable local lens of the one tree, evaluates Nopales against it offline, and reconciles with the server on reconnect. The server keeps the corpus, the data plane authority, and the merge index; the client keeps a working copy and the interactive surface. Single-user per client; the server stays multi-user.")

  (subsection positioning
   (doc "What local-first is and is not, relative to remote-eval, one-tree, and branching")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         IS:
           - A MODE of the Prim_call seam and the one tree, not a new
             product. remote-eval proxies DB prims over HTTP to a
             remote store; local-first resolves them against a LOCAL
             lens and syncs the lens on reconnect. Same interpreter,
             same rim abstraction, different backing for the data
             plane between syncs.
           - A BRANCH OF THE ONE TREE whose root lives on a client.
             This is what reopens remote-eval's "no second store on
             the client" hard-out honestly: the hard-out was written
             against an UNCOORDINATED client copy (a second source of
             truth with no merge semantics). A lens is a GOVERNED copy
             — it is a branch, and branches already have merge
             semantics (branching.borg three-way tree-diff). The lens
             is not a second store; it is a remote branch of the one
             store.
           - The generalization remote-eval was deliberately scoped
             NOT to be. remote-eval's v0 is online-remote (local
             compute, every DB hit crosses the wire); local-first is
             offline-capable (local lens, DB hits resolve locally
             until sync). The placement heuristic
             (remote-eval.placement) is shared: pure compute stays
             local in both; the difference is whether DB reads cross
             the wire per-hit (remote-eval) or read the local lens
             (local-first) until reconnect.

         IS NOT:
           - A second source of truth. The lens is a branch; the
             server is the integration point and the merge authority.
             Quit all clients; the server's tree is whole.
           - A Nopales fork. Same interpreter, same reader, same
             special forms (inherited from remote-eval.substrate and
             lisp-interpreter.borg).
           - Multi-user on the client. One client = one user's lens.
           - A relaxation of single-writer-per-lens. Each client is
             the SOLE writer of its own lens. Multi-writer reappears
             only at MERGE — exactly where three-way tree-diff already
             lives. This is git's argument, and it holds here.
    |))))

  (subsection lens-model
   (doc "A local lens is a branch checked out to a client root. Fork = a pad detaching; sync = grafting back. Single-writer is preserved per-lens.")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/store.ml image/lib/tree_store.ml image/lib/crud.ml image/lib/prims_db.ml remote/lib/lens_store.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         A CLONE IS A FORK. Checking out a local lens forks the
         user's current branch into a client-owned lens
         (e.g. admin/personal -> admin/laptop-2026-08-14). The fork
         is a branch like any other: a pointer into the one tree
         (one-tree.branching-interaction). The client materializes
         the lens subtree to its local substrate (filesystem — see
         local-substrate) and evaluates against it.

         SINGLE-WRITER PER LENS, PRESERVED. The one-tree invariant
         ("one unix image serializes structural mutation; no
         merge-on-write races inside a commit") is preserved PER
         LENS. Each client is the sole writer of its own lens, so
         local writes need no concurrency machinery — a write is a
         local commit, atomicity is the substrate's. Multi-writer
         reappears only at SYNC, when the lens merges back to the
         server branch. That is exactly where three-way tree-diff
         (branching.borg, one-tree.branching-interaction) already
         lives. Local-first does not invent a new concurrency model;
         it adds a second site for the existing merge operation.

         THE MERGE IS AUTOMATIC AND INVISIBLE IN THE COMMON CASE.
         "Each clone is its own branch" and "constant merges" are the
         SAME model, not alternatives — the branch is what makes the
         merges safe, and the merges are cheap because a solo user's
         lens rarely conflicts with its own server branch:
           - DATA is append-mostly and single-writer per user: a new
             todo on the laptop and a new todo on the server are
             disjoint nodes; the merge is a clean union, not a
             conflict.
           - CODE on a personal garden is effectively single-writer:
             you are the only editor of your own defs. A merge is a
             fast-forward unless you edited the SAME def on two
             devices before syncing — the rare, real conflict.
         The branch is a CORRECTNESS MECHANISM, not UX ceremony. The
         user's mental model is "my branch, synced across my devices";
         fork-on-clone and merge-on-sync are implementation.

         MULTI-DEVICE = STAR TOPOLOGY. Each device is its own lens
         (admin/laptop, admin/phone, ...) rendezvousing at the server
         branch (admin/personal) and the user's data subtree
         (data.u.admin). The server is the integration point. This is
         the simplest multi-device topology and it falls out of the
         branch model without addition.

         CODE VS DATA SYNC POLICY. The one tree has two storage
         shapes, and they sync slightly differently — same algorithm
         (three-way tree-diff over subtrees, keyed on node hashes),
         different landing policy:
           - CODE (b.<branch>.{fn,type,route,pkg}.*) is branch-scoped.
             A clone forks a code lens; sync merges lens -> server
             branch by moving the branch pointer (the existing
             promote/merge primitive, branching.borg).
           - DATA (data.u.<user>.<type>.<id>) is user-owned and
             BRANCH-FREE (the 2026-08-02 /data revision). There is no
             branch pointer to move; sync reconciles the user's data
             subtree in place against the server's copy, server-
             mediated. data.shared.* is read-mostly seed; clients
             pull it but propose merges into it (the existing
             "explicit copy/move into data.shared" rule), preserving
             server-sole-writer for shared content.
         One algorithm, two policies for where the merged result
         lands. Both are subtree hash-diff + reconcile; neither
         invents a new primitive.

         PLAN PASS (campaign 8.0, 2026-12) — lens fork CONTRACT pinned
         for the 8.1 make. A lens being "a branch checked out to a
         client root" is made concrete as a CHILD BRANCH of the
         checked-out branch:
           - FORK SURFACE. A clone forks the user's current branch
             into a client-owned lens via the EXISTING (fork ...)
             primitive / Store.fork_branch (branching.personal-
             workspace, one-tree P5 EMPTY-LENS fork — copy-on-fork,
             parent pointer only). The lens is an empty child of the
             checked-out branch; the server branch stays the parent.
             Naming: <owner>/lens-<device>[-<date>] (e.g.
             admin/personal -> admin/lens-laptop-2026-08-14).
           - MATERIALIZE. The client materializes the lens to its
             local substrate (local-substrate) and evaluates against
             it: local DB prims resolve against the lens files; the
             server is only the merge/index authority.
           - SINGLE-WRITER PER LENS, PINS TO THE BRANCH MODEL. The
             server NEVER writes into a client lens except via sync/
             merge. Local writes are commits on the lens; atomicity is
             the substrate's (rename(2)). The branch is what makes the
             merges safe — no new concurrency model.
           - LANDING POLICY (pinned). CODE sync merges lens ->
             server branch by moving the branch pointer (the existing
             promote / merge primitive). DATA sync reconciles the
             user's data subtree IN PLACE against the server's copy
             (data is branch-free; there is no pointer to move). Both
             are subtree hash-diff + reconcile; neither a new prim.
    |))))
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "lens/fork + remote/lib/lens_store.ml fork_lens_name name a lens branch <owner>/lens-<device>[-<date>] as a NAMED CHILD of the checked-out branch (empty-lens fork: parent pointer only, zero own rows, resolves through ancestry)")
    (smoke "the fork is single-writer; the server branch stays the parent (verified: lens-branch parent_id = checked-out branch)"))

  (subsection replication-units
   (doc "What travels to a client: the user's data subtree + the checked-out code lens. The isolation boundary IS the replication boundary.")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/crud.ml image/lib/prims_db.ml remote/lib/lens_store.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE /data REVISION EARNS ITS SECOND SALARY. The 2026-08-02
         user-owned data placement (data.u.<user>.* + data.shared.*)
         was made to fix MULTI-USER ISOLATION — a session sees only
         its own data.u.<user> plus data.shared. It turns out to also
         fix the REPLICATION UNIT: the subtree a client replicates is
         exactly data.u.<user>.* (that user's rows) plus data.shared.*
         (the read-mostly seed). The isolation boundary and the
         replication boundary are the SAME SHAPE — not a coincidence,
         but the tree being honest about ownership. A subtree scoped
         to one user for reads is the right subtree to copy to that
         user's device.

         WHAT A CLIENT REPLICATES:
           - data.u.<user>.<type>.<id>  — the user's own data (full)
           - data.shared.<type>.<id>   — shared/seed content (read-mostly)
           - b.<lens>.{fn,type,route,pkg}.* — the forked code lens
         NOTHING ELSE. Another user's data.u.<other>.* never
         replicates to this client — not by a policy added for
         local-first, but by the path-prefix confinement that already
         governs reads (one-tree RLS floor). The client cannot ask
         for, and the server cannot send, a subtree the client's
         identity cannot read. Replication is authorized reads,
         batched.

         CODE LENS SCOPE. The code lens replicated is the forked
         branch and its ancestry (defs resolve through inheritance,
         branching.borg). A client checking out admin/personal
         replicates personal's overrides plus the system/main
         ancestry they resolve through — the same env a server-side
         eval would build, materialized locally. Inheritance is
         read-time resolution (one-tree tree-model); the client
         stores the pinned overrides and resolves ancestors per the
         cache policy (remote-eval.caching generalizes).

         NO CROSS-USER REPLICATION. Because replication is the
         authorized-read subtree, a compromised client that replays
         its sync token cannot exfiltrate another user's data — it
         can only request what its identity could read in a normal
         session. The trust boundary for local-first is the SAME
         trust boundary the online session already has, restated as
         "what subtree may replicate." This is why local-first does
         not inherit a new leak surface from remote-eval's browser/
         WASM trust punt: the browser punt is about EXECUTING
         untrusted corpus code in a browser; local-first executes
         the user's OWN corpus on the user's OWN device, which is the
         trust relationship the online image already assumes.

         PLAN PASS (campaign 8.0, 2026-12) — REPLICATION SUBTREE PINNED
         for the 8.1 make. The replication unit is exactly the
         authorized-read subtree:
           data.u.<user>.*                        the user's own data (full)
           data.shared.*                          read-mostly seed
           b.<lens>.{fn,type,route,pkg}.*         forked code lens (pinned at fork)
           PLUS the branch ancestry the lens resolves through (the
           system/main + parent overrides a server-side eval of the
           lens would build — materialized locally per inheritance
           read-time resolution).
         NOTHING outside it. Path-prefix confinement (one-tree RLS
         floor) is the replication boundary; the client cannot ask
         for, and the server cannot send, a subtree the identity
         cannot read in a normal session. Replication is authorized
         reads, BATCHED.

         SYNC-NEGOTIATION HANDLE (the coordinate the manifest exchange
         keys on). The client records the (branch, generation) it last
         synced at; that snapshot is the merge base. This is NOT a new
         counter — it is the SAME migration-017 branch_generations
         coordinate tree-time promoted to the head/gen clock
         (gen-now / Store.current_generation) and remote-eval.caching
         keys its def cache on. One counter, three consumers
         (def-cache freshness, reads-as-of-gen, last-synced merge
         base). A sync round negotiates (path, hash) manifests and
         offers (branch, generation) as the common ancestor.
    |))))
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "replication/unit + remote/lib/lens_store.ml in_replication_unit materialize EXACTLY data.u.<user>.* + data.shared.* + b.<lens>.* + branch ancestry (path-prefix RLS confinement is the replication boundary; another user's data.u.<other>.* never replicates)")
    (smoke "image/test/local_first_prims_test.ml: lens/fork creates the empty-lens named child; replication/unit returns a well-formed manifest"))

  (subsection local-substrate
   (doc "The filesystem as lens root. No second query engine; the server is the index.")
   (status implemented)
   (implements remote/lib/lens_store.ml remote/lib/remote.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE FILESYSTEM, NOT SQLITE. A local lens is a directory tree
         of node-envelope JSON files, one file per node, at its path:

           <lens-root>/
             b.admin.laptop.fn.todo-get.json
             b.admin.laptop.type.note.json
             data.u.admin.todo/<uuid>.json
             data.shared.flash-deck/<uuid>.json

         Each file is the node envelope from one-tree.tree-model
         ({v,kind,path,type,body,author,doc,hash,created_at,
         updated_at}). The filesystem IS the path index: subtree
         queries are readdir/find; uniqueness is the filesystem; the
         envelope is a JSON file. There is no engine to align with
         Postgres, no second schema, no second set of migrations —
         the thing the remote-eval hard-out was trying to prevent
         (a second store) does not arise, because the lens is not a
         store, it is a materialized subtree.

         WHY NOT SQLITE. SQLite imports a second query engine, a
         second schema, and a JSON1-vs-jsonb semantic divergence that
         must be kept aligned with Postgres by hand. Its entire reason
         to exist — concurrent ACID over a shared file — is unused:
         a local lens has ONE writer (the client), so atomicity is
         rename(2) and there is no concurrency machinery to justify.
         SQLite is the answer for a system with concurrent local
         writers; this system has none.

         THE VAULT IS ALREADY FILES. pp-sync ships libs/*.pp, /code
         serves PP_SOURCE_ROOT, backups are directory dumps. The
         filesystem substrate IS the vault format — a local lens and
         a pp-sync snapshot are the same physical thing. A lens is
         inspectable with cat, jq, grep, diff, git. This is the
         Smalltalk/Pharo filetree feeling, honest: the image as an
         inspectable directory.

         THE SERVER IS THE INDEX. A local lens is a WORKING COPY, not
         a query engine. Indexed reads over the whole corpus
         (tree/find-by with a body predicate, rows with a filter,
         list-kind over a large type) route to the server — the
         remote-eval placement heuristic, inherited. The lens holds
         the working set (open notes, current todos, the def being
         edited); the server holds the index over the corpus. Pure
         compute and working-set reads stay local; analytical reads
         cross the wire. Offline mode degrades gracefully on the
         analytical axis (indexed queries unavailable) while staying
         full-power on the working-set axis — the right failure
         shape for offline.

         SUBSTRATE INTERFACE, NOT SUBSTRATE MANDATE. The spec pins
         the interface (path-addressed node get/put/subtree-list +
         envelope + hash) and names the filesystem as the v1
         substrate. A content-addressed blob store is the natural v2
         once content-addressing lands in the tree proper
         (one-tree.tree-model "content-addressing later"); the
         interface is stable across that swap. SQLite is not a named
         future substrate — if a lens ever needs real local
         indexing, the answer is a content-addressed store or a
         remote call to the server's index, not a second relational
         engine on the client.
    |)))
   (details
    (doc (|
         8.2 MAKE (campaign 8, 2026-12). The substrate interface
         (path-addressed get/put/subtree-list + envelope + hash) was
         already in lens_store.ml (8.1) ; this make COMPLETED the
         replication-unit coverage the local-substrate stanza names:

           - CODE-LENS MATERIALIZATION. [materialize_code_node] writes a
             code-node envelope at its b.<branch>.<kind>.<name> tree path
             (kind in {fn,type,route,pkg}) carrying the evaluable def
             source (the (define …) form) + doc, same one-tree envelope
             shape + content hash as datum nodes — so sync's three-way
             tree-diff keys on the same [hash] for code.

           - ANCESTRY MATERIALIZATION (read-time inheritance).
             [record_ancestry] / [ancestry] store the self-first
             (closest-wins) branch chain (lens, parent, …, system/main)
             as <root>/__ancestry.json — the SAME override order
             store.branch_chain / function_defs use. [resolve_def]
             walks it, returning the closest b.<branch>.<kind>.<name>
             envelope (None on miss) — offline read-time inheritance
             per one-tree tree-model, no server round trip.

           - CLIENT WIRING (evaluates against the local substrate).
             remote/lib/remote.ml takes an optional [~lens_lookup]
             (substrate def source); when set, [fetch_def] resolves
             FROM THE SUBSTRATE FIRST and only proxies
             [function-source] on a miss — the offline-first def path
             of the lens-model materialization.

           - (PATH, HASH) MANIFEST. [node_manifest] walks the lens root
             and returns every node's (tree path, envelope hash) — the
             exact manifest the sync stanza's exchange negotiates on.

         NOT in this make (kept open, owned by the sync stanza): the
         bidirectional manifest exchange + three-way merge against the
         server (image/lib/sync.ml, the actual-8.3 make). Local-first is
         not shipped end-to-end until 8.3 lands; this closes the
         substrate's own coverage so 8.3 diffs against a complete
         manifest source.
    |)))
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "remote/test/lens_store_test.ml: the local-substrate group (path_to_file, materialize_and_get, put_body_hash, subtree_list, code_kind_path, materialize_code_and_resolve, node_manifest, lens_fetch_first) — 10/10 green: code-lens nodes materialize at b.<branch>.<kind>.<name> with an evaluable src; ancestry records + round-trips self-first chain; resolve_def is closest-wins (a lens override beats its ancestry copy) and inherits a def present only on ancestry; the (path, hash) manifest lists every node (code AND datum) and excludes the __ancestry metadata; Remote.fetch_def consults the substrate FIRST (a transport that raises is never touched on a substrate hit)")))

  (subsection sync
   (doc "Sync = three-way tree-diff (branching.borg / one-tree.branching-interaction), keyed on subtree node hashes. The exchange is the git protocol in miniature.")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/tree_store.ml image/lib/sync.ml image/test/sync_test.ml branching.borg)
   (verify
    (doc (|
         Sync = the server-side merge authority over tree_nodes, keyed on
         the same subtree node content hash the client lens substrate
         computes offline (one-tree.tree-model) — ONE hash scheme, both
         sides. image/test/sync_test.ml covers the pure core: manifest
         classification (same / client-only / server-only / diverge = the
         exchange's conflict DETECTION at node granularity) and the
         three-way merge planner (base = last-synced, ours = server,
         theirs = client) with every outcome (unchanged, theirs_add,
         ours_add, theirs_mod, ours_mod, identical, conflict, theirs_del,
         ours_del) + the actionable subset + JSON rendering. The
         sync/plan prim surfaces server_manifest / classify / plan_of
         (with an optional last-synced base manifest — the tree-time gen
         coordinate as merge base). The DB-backed harness
         (server_manifest / apply_client_node / commit) is exercised per
         the coexistence / deployment passes.
    |)))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ONE ALGORITHM, ALREADY SPECIFIED. Sync is the three-way
         tree-diff that one-tree.branching-interaction already plans
         for promote/merge. Subtree content hashes (one-tree.tree-
         model: hash over canonical body, root hashes compose from
         leaves) give CONFLICT DETECTION at node granularity.
         Generation counters (remote-eval.caching) generalize from
         one-way cache invalidation to bidirectional sync
         negotiation. Nothing new is invented: local-first sync is
         the merge primitive with a second consumer.

         THE EXCHANGE. A sync round is the git protocol in miniature:
           1. Client sends its (path, hash) manifest for the lens
              subtree(s).
           2. Server compares against its own subtree hashes; reports
              which paths differ or are missing on each side.
           3. Each side sends the node BODIES (envelopes) the other
              is missing or has stale.
           4. Server applies the three-way merge (common ancestor =
              last-synced generation) and returns the merged subtree
              state; client applies the same merge locally.
         The envelope hash is the negotiation; the file is the
         transfer. No second query engine in the loop.

         CODE SYNC. The forked code lens (b.<lens>.*) merges to the
         server branch (b.<user>.personal) by moving the branch
         pointer — the existing promote/merge primitive. Single-
         writer per user's personal code means this is usually a fast
         forward; conflict only on the same def edited on two
         devices.

         DATA SYNC. data.u.<user>.* reconciles in place against the
         server's copy (no branch pointer — data is branch-free).
         Append-mostly user data means disjoint new nodes union
         cleanly; conflict only on the same node edited on two
         devices. data.shared.* is pulled (server-sole-writer);
         client proposals into shared go through an explicit
         merge-request, not direct write.

         LAST-SYNCED AS THE COMMON ANCESTOR. Three-way merge needs a
         common ancestor. The client records the (branch, generation)
         it last synced at; that snapshot is the merge base. This is
         the remote-eval.caching generation counter, repurposed from
         "is my cache fresh" to "what was our last common state."
         One counter, two uses.
    |))))

  (subsection the-glochids
   (doc "The honest open work: conflict RESOLUTION policy, offline identity, semantics skew, transactions-under-sync. Detection is free; resolution is not.")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         THE TREE DETECTS; IT DOES NOT RESOLVE. Subtree hashes give
         node-granular conflict DETECTION (these two nodes diverged
         since the common ancestor). They do not give a RESOLUTION
         policy. Naming the policy is the real research-shaped gap —
         the pads graft easily most of the time; the glochids bite at
         sync. Candidates, in increasing ambition:
           - LAST-WRITER-WINS per node (updated_at arbitrates). Cheap,
             loses concurrent edits to the same node. Acceptable for
             data where the rare same-node concurrent edit is a user
             error anyway.
           - SEMANTIC MERGE per type (a type declares its merge
             strategy: todo status is LWW, note body is
             three-way-text-merge, a counter field is sum). Uses
             one-tree typed-nodes — the type declaration carries the
             merge policy. Most honest; most work.
           - CRDT-ISH field types for hot fields (a counter that
             sums, a set that unions). Opt-in per field via the type
             declaration. Heaviest; only where worth it.
         v0 is LWW-per-node with a conflict SURFACED to the user (no
         silent loss — the obituary rule, habitat-workflow, applies
         to sync losses too). Semantic merge is the v1 ambition and
         the place typed-nodes earns a third salary.

         OFFLINE IDENTITY. remote-eval: "identity is server-asserted,
         never client-asserted." Offline edits must assert author
         locally and reconcile at sync. The per-node author field +
         branch ownership give the audit trail; the mechanism is
         durable client credentials (a signed sync token / device
         key) presented at sync, with the server asserting the
         identity onto the merged nodes. The client never CLAIMS an
         author it does not hold — it stamps its credential; the
         server validates and attributes. This is a real trust-
         boundary change from online (where the session asserts per
         request) and it inherits remote-eval's browser/WASM trust
         punt as an open question for untrusted-client execution.

         SEMANTICS VERSION SKEW. remote-eval hard-constraint already
         flags "a story for branch pinned to Nopales-semantics vN is
         required once the corpus is the product." Local-first makes
         it acute: an offline client runs old interpreter semantics
         against a corpus that may have evolved at sync. The lens
         pins a (branch, generation, semantics-version) snapshot
         (remote-eval.stepping's "pin a snapshot and replay" is the
         substrate). At sync, a semantics-version mismatch is a
         merge-gate event: the client must rebase to the server's
         semantics before merging edits, or the server re-evaluates
         the client's def source under current semantics. The honest
         rule: defs are durable DATA (source), so the server can
         always re-eval a client's def source under current
         semantics — the skew is in BEHAVIOR the client observed
         offline, not in the source. Behavior skew is surfaced, not
         silently merged.

         TRANSACTIONS UNDER SYNC. Online, the remote-eval hard
         constraint stands: (with-transaction ...) holds
         (select-for-update) locks that cannot span HTTP, so
         transaction-wrapped exprs execute server-side. Offline,
         the client is sole writer of its lens — local transactions
         work as-is (single writer, locks trivial, the lens substrate
         provides atomicity via rename). At SYNC, concurrent local
         transactions become MERGE CONFLICTS, not lock contention —
         the hard constraint MOVES, it does not vanish. It stays true
         online; offline it becomes a merge problem this subsection
         owns. A transaction that must be atomic across sync (rare —
         most txns are within a single editing session) is surfaced
         as a conflict on all its constituent nodes rather than
         silently partial-merged.

         STATUS (8.4 reconcile): CONFLICT DETECTION IS SHIPPED.
         Campaign 8.3 (sync subsection, implemented) delivers the
         node-granular divergence detection this subsection's thesis
         names: image/lib/sync.ml manifests path,hash deltas,
         classifies them, and three-way-merges (base = last-synced
         generation, ours = server, theirs = client) to a plan whose
         conflict outcome is surfaced REPORT-ONLY — the obituary rule
         applied to sync losses, exactly the "v0 surfaces; it does not
         silently resolve" stance. What is NOT yet shipped is the
         RESOLUTION half: the LWW-per-node v0 policy is named here but
         not applied anywhere — sync/plan emits a plan with conflict
         outcomes and stops; there is no sync/commit prim applying a
         plan's Theirs_* ops, and no automatic LWW arbitration of a
         same-node divergent pair at sync (the transport-side follow-up
         recorded in the 8.3 notes). OFFLINE IDENTITY, SEMANTICS
         VERSION SKEW, and TRANSACTIONS UNDER SYNC also remain unbuilt
         (no durable device-signing credential, no branch generation
         semantics snapshot pin carried by a lens, no sync-time rebase
         gate or post-merge re-eval). Subsection stays planned; the
         open work is resolution + identity + skew + txns-under-sync,
         not detection.
    |))))

  (subsection phasing
   (doc "Read-only offline first (achievable, defers the glochids); write-capable lenses with automatic merge second")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PHASE 1 — READ-ONLY OFFLINE. A client pins a (branch,
         generation) snapshot, materializes it to the filesystem
         lens, and evaluates read-only against it offline. No local
         writes; on reconnect, the client re-syncs the snapshot
         (cheap — discard and re-pull, since it made no edits). This
         is much closer to shipped: it reuses remote-eval.caching's
         generation pin and the filesystem substrate, with NO merge
         machinery. It delivers most of the local-first FEEL (no
         spinners on reads, offline read access, instant open) without
         the merge-conflict policy. The debugger substrate
         (remote-eval.stepping) works against a read-only lens
         unchanged.

         PHASE 2 — WRITE-CAPABLE LENSES. Fork-on-clone, local writes,
         automatic merge on sync (lens-model + sync). LWW-per-node
         conflict resolution with surfaced conflicts (the-glochids
         v0). This is the full local-first design. The glochids
         (semantic merge, offline identity, semantics skew) mature
         within phase 2, not before it.

         NOT A PHASE GATE. Phase 1 is a shippable milestone, not a
         prerequisite — the phase 2 design does not depend on phase 1
         being shipped first. But phase 1 is the honest first cut
         because it defers the one piece of real research (conflict
         resolution policy) while delivering the working-copy feel.

         STATUS (8.4 reconcile): PHASE 1 READ-ONLY OFFLINE LANDED IN
         SUBSTRATE. The branch generation snapshot coordinate is the
         migration-017 / gen-now clock (remote-eval.caching — one
         counter, three consumers); the filesystem lens substrate
         (remote/lib/lens_store.ml, 8.2) materializes a lens root
         path-addressed get/put/subtree-list + envelope + hash as
         rename(2) atomicity; materialize_code_node + ancestry
         resolution let a client eval defs from the substrate offline
         (offline-first: fetch_def resolves from the substrate BEFORE
         proxying). What is NOT shipped is a user-facing read-only
         offline FLOW (a CLI/app that pins a snapshot, materializes,
         evaluates read-only, and re-syncs by discard-and-re-pull on
         reconnect) — the primitives exist, the flow does not, so
         Phase 1 as a shippable milestone is still open. PHASE 2
         WRITE-CAPABLE AUTOMATIC MERGE LANDED IN AUTHORITY, OPEN IN
         TRANSPORT+RESOLUTION. The server-side three-way merge
         authority (8.3 sync) + lens/fork + replication/unit (8.1) +
         the substrate give the merge MACHINERY, but automatic merge
         end-to-end needs (a) the sync/commit prim applying a plan's
         Theirs_* ops from client bodies + the HTTP rendezvous
         shipping manifests/bodies (recorded as the 8.3
         transport-side follow-up, not yet built) and (b) the-glochids
         v0 LWW policy actually applied (not shipped). Subsection
         stays planned; the open work is the Phase-2 tail (transport +
         resolution) plus the Phase-1 end-user flow.
    |))))

  (subsection non-goals
   (doc "What local-first is NOT")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         - NOT a second store. The lens is a branch of the one tree;
           the server is the integration point and merge authority.
         - NOT multi-user on the client. One client = one user's
           lens, same as remote-eval.
         - NOT a Nopales fork. Same interpreter, same reader, same
           special forms (remote-eval.substrate).
         - NOT a relaxation of single-writer-per-lens. Each client is
           sole writer of its own lens; multi-writer appears only at
           merge.
         - NOT automatic conflict RESOLUTION. The tree DETECTS
           conflicts (hashes); resolution is a policy (the-glochids).
           v0 surfaces; it does not silently resolve.
          - Data lens versioning (IMPLEMENTED v0, 2026-08-23 make).
            data.u.<user>.* stays single-head and branch-free (this
            property is preserved). An opt-in data.l.<lens>.<user>.*
            namespace adds deliberate data forks that diverge and
            merge back via LWW (v0; three-way tree-diff is v1).
            The local-first lens materializes and syncs the user's
            data subtree; the data lens fork makes that first-class.
         - NOT a second transport. HTTP+JSON is the only wire in v0
           (inherited from remote-eval). The filesystem is the local
           substrate, not a wire.
         - NOT a browser/WASM client. Executing a user's corpus in a
           browser is a trust boundary local-first inherits from
           remote-eval's punt and does not reopen; local-first runs
           on a client the user controls (laptop, phone), where the
           trust relationship is "my code on my device."
    |))))
 )
)
